# Target C-II — optimized three-cut kernel cusp has at most `1083` exceptional diameter rows

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only quantitative strengthening  
**Scope:** full-core Type-II branch with `t=(q-k)/D<=1.844` on the optimized good smallest-leaf backbone  
**Dependencies:** `target_c_three_cut_kernel_cusp_2026-09-15.md`; `target_c_t1844_retention_2217_2026-09-15.md`; optimized adjacent backbone density; exact laminar interval support  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_three_cut_kernel_cusp_1084.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The original three-cut kernel theorem chose three cuts using the older balanced adjacent-boundary averaging constant.  That was enough to isolate the first-order diameter cusp, but it left the coarse bound

\[
|H_{exc}|<42400+o(1).
\]

The current `t<=1.844` stack is much tighter.  The retained-mass theorem gives

\[
z>2.217-o(1),
\]

which lowers the full actual diameter-edge density, while the optimized fan threshold gives a good-leaf path of density

\[
p=0.00682246453482\ldots .
\]

Applying the same two-boundary averaging to those sharper inputs reduces the exact three-cut exceptional set to at most `1083` rows, while preserving inverse-`D` switching KKT mass and inverse-`D` phase variation on the same triple.

### Theorem K1083

Every sufficiently large survivor in the stated branch has three consecutive smallest-leaf O--O cuts

\[
\tau_i,\tau_{i+1},\tau_{i+2}
\]

and a nonzero first-order kernel coefficient vector `c` with

\[
c_i u_i+c_{i+1}u_{i+1}+c_{i+2}u_{i+2}=0
\]

such that the exceptional diameter set

\[
H_{exc}
:=H_{sw,i}\cup H_{sw,i+1}
\]

satisfies, for all sufficiently large members,

\[
\boxed{|H_{exc}|\le1083.}
\tag{K83.1}
\]

On the same triple,

\[
\boxed{
\sum_{h\in H_{exc}}\beta_h=O(D^{-1}),
}
\tag{K83.2}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
 d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_i,u_{i+1})
+d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_{i+1},u_{i+2})
=O(D^{-1}).
}
\tag{K83.3}

All qualitative conclusions of the parent three-cut theorem remain unchanged.  In particular, every active diameter row outside `H_exc` has zero first derivative exactly under the kernel shear; canonical C65 forces the finite exceptional cusp to contain positive-stress rows of both derivative signs; and whenever the three train vectors admit exact `G=0` integration, only these at most `1083` old diameter constraints move at all.

---

## 1. Sharper global switch-count budget

The exact `z>2.217` certificate gives the outward full-diameter-edge bound

\[
{h_H\over D}
<1.848545512817+o(1).
\]

Use the simpler safe rational value

\[
\boxed{{h_H\over D}<1.848546+o(1).}
\tag{K83.4}
\]

Along any selected laminar dual path, each diameter interval starts and stops at most twice.  Hence

\[
\sum_j N_j\le2h_H.
\tag{K83.5}
\]

For a three-cut window define

\[
U_j:=N_j+N_{j+1}.
\]

Every internal boundary count appears in at most two such windows, so

\[
\boxed{
\sum_jU_j
\le2\sum_jN_j
<4(1.848546)D+o(D).
}
\tag{K83.6}
\]

---

## 2. Optimized path density

Use the already-certified count-focused fan parameters

\[
\lambda=0.6404,
\qquad
d_{low}=10^{-4}.
\]

They produce a single center train crossing a consecutive good-leaf path of length

\[
m>pD-o(D),
\]

with exact rational density

\[
p={461524\over67647695}
=0.00682246453482\ldots .
\tag{K83.7}
\]

Thus the count-only average two-boundary exceptional size is below

\[
{4(1.848546)\over p}
=1083.7995511\ldots .
\tag{K83.8}
\]

The integer threshold `1084` is therefore strictly above the average.

---

## 3. Keep KKT switching mass and phase variation small on the same triple

The laminar interval budgets give

\[
\sum_j B_j\le2,
\]

so the two-boundary windows obey

\[
\sum_j D(B_j+B_{j+1})\le4D.
\tag{K83.9}
\]

The optimized path construction retains the global phase budget

\[
\sum_jD(q_j+q_{j+1})
\le {2C_{phase}\over d_{low}}D+o(D),
\]

hence

\[
\sum_jD[(q_j+q_{j+1})+(q_{j+1}+q_{j+2})]
\le {4C_{phase}\over d_{low}}D+o(D).
\tag{K83.10}
\]

Use the Pareto union bound with thresholds

\[
N=1084,
\qquad
L_\beta=L_\phi=10^{12}.
\]

The exact companion certificate bounds the bad-window densities by

\[
0.00682120295203\ldots,
\]

\[
4\times10^{-12},
\]

and

\[
2.1266929828\times10^{-7},
\]

respectively.  Their sum is below `p` by

\[
>1.04\times10^{-6}.
\]

Hence one consecutive triple satisfies simultaneously

\[
U_j<1084+o(1),
\]

\[
D(B_j+B_{j+1})<10^{12}+o(1),
\]

and the analogous `10^12+o(1)` bound for the total two-gap phase cost.

For sufficiently large sources, integrality gives (K83.1), while the latter two estimates give (K83.2)--(K83.3).

---

## 4. Updated finite three-cut normal form

The live exact three-cut obstruction is therefore

```text
three consecutive smallest-leaf O-O cuts
+ exact nonzero planar kernel c_1 u_1+c_2 u_2+c_3 u_3=0
+ at most 1083 exceptional actual diameter rows
+ exceptional beta mass O(1/D)
+ total two-gap phase variation O(1/D)
+ every nonexceptional diameter row has zero first derivative exactly
+ C65 => the exceptional positive-stress cusp has both derivative signs
+ exact finite G=0 localization except for the classified same-oriented parallel branch.
```

The one/two-bar adjacent-strip reaction theorems are stronger for tangent and force certificates.  The `1083` bound serves a different purpose: it controls the **complete active set that can move** under the exact three-cut finite motion, which is needed for nonlinear replacement or second-order analysis.

No C-II closure is claimed.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only quantitative strengthening of the exact three-cut kernel cusp from `<42400` exceptional rows to at most `1083`. C-II and Level I remain open.
